WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.280
Pennywise may not be the scariest thing in
Derry, Maine, after all.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:08.560
Audiences who have taken in ​It: Chapter
Two​ were a little surprised to see a familiar

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.840
face pop up in the film: none other than Stephen
King himself, who appeared as the proprietor

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.800
of the store Secondhand Rose.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.020
Screenwriter Gary Dauberman revealed that
he wrote the part of Secondhand Rose's owner

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:24.279
with King in mind, but stopped just short
of actually spelling out his preference in

00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:25.279
the screenplay.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.279
He told Inverse:

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:31.239
"I wrote him into the script just along the
lines of 'looks like Stephen King' early on

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:31.840
in the drafts.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.180
You know, just kind of planting the seed."

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:38.760
Dauberman was unsure if King would be willing
to take the time to cameo in the film.

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:44.260
Luckily, while King's high-profile cameo appearances
may have come in the '80s and '90s, he's always

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.460
been pretty game to pop up in adaptations
of his material.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.000
It's just that in recent years, he's mostly
stuck to the small screen.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.800
King's very first movie appearance was in
a film not even adapted from one of his novels:

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.860
it was in the bizarre 1981 fantasy flick Knightriders,
which was directed by his friend George A.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:02.560
Romero.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.970
King and Romero would go on to collaborate
on the excellent horror anthology film Creepshow

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:11.740
the following year, and in that film, King
didn't just cameo; he actually starred in

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.620
one of its segments, "The Lonesome Death of
Jordy Verrill."

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:20.020
"You get in that water, Jordy, you might as
well sign your death warrant."

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.340
"I'm a goner already, Daddy."

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.080
Since then, King has turned in cameos in no
fewer than fifteen films and television series.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:35.060
He cameoed in 1986's Maximum Overdrive, which
is also the only film that King ever directed.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:41.840
After that, he appeared in 1987's Creepshow
2, 1989's Pet Sematary, and plenty more.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:46.040
Dauberman related that he was content to just
keep his fingers crossed and hope that King

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:47.380
would agree to the cameo.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.820
However, Andy Muschietti revealed that he
was down to do a little more bargaining in

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:53.100
the interest of making it happen.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.340
The director told Games Radar that:

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:59.819
"We had an increasingly good relationship
with King after he saw the first movie.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:03.340
He became like a friend, and I wanted him
to participate a little more in this one as

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.820
he did not participate [in] the first movie
at all.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.819
I didn't want him to intervene in the creation,
and he's very clear he doesn't want to mess

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.860
with the people adapting his work, but I still
wanted to keep him in the loop and get his

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:14.360
thoughts."

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.140
The director continued that King actually
offered some thoughts that made it to the

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:18.800
big screen:

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.740
"So, I sent him the first draft.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.760
He gave a list of things he would like to
see.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.100
It felt more like a fan, because after reading
it, he was like 'I would like to see Paul

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:27.600
Bunyan'.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.520
So, I was like, 'I'll give you Paul Bunyan.'"

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.230
Muschietti is referring, of course, to a flashback
in which the adult Richie remembers being

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.440
bullied and forced to flee an arcade in his
younger years.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:42.400
Young Richie takes refuge in Derry's park,
where a giant, 20-foot tall statue of Paul

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.040
Bunyan comes terrifyingly to life, chasing
him with its gigantic axe.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.510
With that bargaining chip in place, Muschietti
says he then sprang his request on King.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:56.260
"I asked him to do a cameo and he said, 'Well,
you have to know, I'm a jinx.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:57.840
Every movie I was in bombed.'

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.240
He said it with humor, and I said, 'I think
we're going to break the spell, let's do it.'"

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.500
We know full well that the Master of Horror
is constantly busy conjuring up his latest

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:07.040
nightmare…

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:08.900
"Do you know me?

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.099
It's frightening how many novels of suspense
I've written."

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.780
...but we'd love it if he kept those appearances
coming.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:19.620
Stan Lee is no longer with us, and we can
think of nobody better to claim Stan the Man's

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.310
title of King of Cameos.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:25.780
We'll just have to see if he shows up in Doctor
Sleep, another adaptation of his work, when

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:27.440
it's released in November.

